[medium] internal/service/llm/anthropic.go:142 (correctness)
Retryable Anthropic HTTP responses other than 429 and 529 fall through as the raw SDK error. With SDK retries disabled, a 500/502/503/504 response, and the SDK retryable 408/409 cases, will not wrap ErrOverloaded or another exported sentinel, so the River worker cannot apply the intended retry contract and may treat a transient provider failure as an unknown/non-retryable error.
Suggested fix: In mapAnthropicError, map retryable HTTP statuses such as 408, 409, and >=500 to ErrOverloaded while keeping 429 as ErrRateLimited and 400 as ErrInvalidOutput; add table-driven tests for at least 500/503 and optionally 408/409.
Guidance: In internal/service/llm/anthropic.go, update mapAnthropicError inside the errors.As(err, &apiErr) switch. Keep the current deliberate mappings unchanged: 401 wraps ErrAuth, 400 wraps ErrInvalidOutput, 429 wraps ErrRateLimited, and 529 wraps ErrOverloaded. Add retryable HTTP statuses to wrap ErrOverloaded: http.StatusRequestTimeout (408), http.StatusConflict (409), and all 5xx responses, including 500, 502, 503, and 504. Because Go switch cases cannot use a range directly, either add explicit cases for the named statuses plus a post-switch `if apiErr.StatusCode >= 500 { return fmt.Errorf("%w: %v", ErrOverloaded, err) }`, or use a switch with boolean conditions (`switch { case apiErr.StatusCode == ... }`). Do not map 400 to ErrOverloaded; ErrInvalidOutput for HTTP 400 is a deliberate design decision. Do not enable SDK retries; option.WithMaxRetries(0) is intentional because River owns retry behavior through exported sentinels. In internal/service/llm/anthropic_test.go, extend TestAnthropicClientErrorMapping with table cases for at least 500 and 503 expecting ErrOverloaded; preferably include 408 and 409 too. Use valid Anthropic error JSON bodies for those statuses so the SDK returns *anthropic.Error and the test exercises mapAnthropicError, not response JSON parsing.

